//@version=6
// MIDAS Bar Streamer — compounds real OHLCV history via the existing webhook.
//
// TradingView forbids automated scraping (screen scraping, API replay,
// scripted logins) of its own data — that rules out unofficial pullers like
// tvdatafeed. alert() is the sanctioned channel: this fires one JSON message
// per closed bar, reusing the same /api/webhook/tradingview/<TOKEN> endpoint
// and gateway already built for MIDAS_Regime_Filter.pine. Point this at
// whatever symbol/timeframe you want to start accumulating — e.g. MGC1! on
// a 1-minute chart — and every bar close lands in the gateway's market_bars
// cache from that point forward. It does not back-fill history; TradingView
// alerts only fire on live and newly-loaded bars, never on the past.
//
// Setup:
//   1. Add this to a chart at the symbol/timeframe you want to capture.
//   2. Right-click -> Add alert -> Condition: this indicator -> "Any alert()
//      function call" -> Trigger: Once Per Bar Close.
//   3. Webhook URL: the same https://<app>/api/webhook/tradingview/<TOKEN>
//      used for the regime filter. Leave the alert Message box EMPTY.
//   4. One alert per symbol/timeframe you want streamed — a free/basic plan
//      caps concurrent alerts, so this is deliberately a separate, minimal
//      indicator rather than bolted onto the regime filter.
indicator("MIDAS Bar Streamer", overlay=true)

// barstate.isconfirmed gates the alert() call to a closed bar's final
// evaluation; alert.freq_once_per_bar_close is belt-and-suspenders so
// TradingView's alert engine dedupes to exactly one fire per bar even if
// the script re-evaluates intrabar.
if barstate.isconfirmed
    alert('{"event":"bar_close"' +
          ',"symbol":"'    + syminfo.ticker + '"' +
          ',"timeframe":"' + timeframe.period + '"' +
          ',"bar_time":'   + str.tostring(time) +
          ',"open":'       + str.tostring(open) +
          ',"high":'       + str.tostring(high) +
          ',"low":'        + str.tostring(low) +
          ',"close":'      + str.tostring(close) +
          ',"volume":'     + str.tostring(volume) +
          '}', alert.freq_once_per_bar_close)

// Purely a data pipe — nothing plotted, nothing else computed.
